					<item>
		<title><![CDATA[Design tokens that survive a redesign]]></title>
		<link>https://wpaxiom.com/design-tokens-survive/</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 05 Sep 2026 19:02:12 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://wpaxiom.com/design-tokens-survive/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/table-of-contents {"eyebrow":"On this page","markerType":"numbered","bgColor":"#FFFFFF","borderColor":"#EBE9F2","borderRadius":"16px","activeColor":"#7C3AED","markerColor":"#7C3AED","maxWidth":"100%","levelH2":true,"levelH3":false} /-->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">A token set is only useful if it outlives the visual direction that produced it. Ours did not, twice. The third attempt has held for two years, and the difference was not tooling.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Name the job, not the look</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Our first palette had a token called <code>purple-600</code>. When the brand moved to a warmer accent, every one of those names became a small lie. We renamed 40 of them in an afternoon and broke three products, because two teams had been using <code>purple-600</code> to mean "interactive" and one had been using it to mean "premium tier".</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">The names that survived the next redesign were the ones describing what a value does. <code>action</code>, <code>surface</code>, <code>border-subtle</code>. You can repaint all three without touching a single component.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/notice {"noticeType":"info","title":"The rename test","message":"Before you add a token, ask whether its name still makes sense if the brand color changes tomorrow. If it doesn't, the name is describing a pixel rather than a purpose.","accentColor":"#7C3AED","bgColor":"#F1EBFE","titleColor":"#17131F","textColor":"#4C4658","borderRadius":"12px","showIcon":true,"iconSlug":"info"} -->
<div class="wp-block-axiom-blocks-notice"><div class="ab-notice__content"><strong class="ab-notice__title">The rename test</strong><div class="ab-notice__message">Before you add a token, ask whether its name still makes sense if the brand color changes tomorrow. If it doesn't, the name is describing a pixel rather than a purpose.</div></div></div>
<!-- /wp:axiom-blocks/notice -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Keep the raw scale underneath</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Semantic names alone are not enough. Somebody always needs the actual hex, usually for a chart or an email template where your CSS never reaches. Two layers solved it: a raw scale nobody references directly, and a semantic layer that points at it. The raw layer is allowed to be boring and numeric. The semantic layer is where the thinking goes.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">What we would skip next time</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">We spent a month building a sync pipeline between the design file and the repository. It broke every time someone renamed a layer, and the failures were silent. A weekly export that a human reviews has caused fewer problems than the automation did, which is not the conclusion we wanted.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[A token set is only useful if it outlives the visual direction that produced it. Here is the structure that tends to hold.]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>268</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-09-05 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-05 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:post_modified><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified>
		<wp:post_modified_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[design-tokens-survive]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
		<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
		<wp:post_type><![CDATA[post]]></wp:post_type>
		<wp:post_password><![CDATA[]]></wp:post_password>
		<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
										<category domain="category" nicename="design"><![CDATA[Design]]></category>
						<wp:postmeta>
		<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[_pingme]]></wp:meta_key>
		<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[1]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
							</item>
					<item>
		<title><![CDATA[Spacing scales, and when to break them]]></title>
		<link>https://wpaxiom.com/spacing-scales-break/</link>
		<pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 19:02:12 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://wpaxiom.com/spacing-scales-break/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Every layout eventually meets a case the scale does not cover. Breaking it deliberately beats drifting off it by accident, and the difference shows up months later when somebody tries to work out why one card has 22px of padding.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Four steps is usually enough</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">We shipped an eleven step scale and used four of them. The other seven existed so that nobody would ever feel constrained, which turned out to be the problem. Given a choice between 20px and 24px, two designers pick differently and neither is wrong, so the page ends up with both.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Cutting to 4, 8, 12, 20, 24 and 40 made the decision automatic. The gaps between steps are large enough that the right one is obvious, and small enough that nothing feels cramped.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">When to break it</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Optical alignment is the honest exception. An icon that measures 24px can still look wrong next to 24px of text, because its visual mass sits inside a transparent box. Nudging it by 2px is not a failure of the scale. Write the reason in a comment and move on.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">The dishonest exception is the one nobody documents. If a value exists because a screenshot needed to fit a slide, it will be copied into three more components before anyone notices.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[Every layout eventually meets a case the scale does not cover. Breaking it deliberately beats drifting off it by accident.]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>269</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-09-04 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-04 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:post_modified><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified>
		<wp:post_modified_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[spacing-scales-break]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
		<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
		<wp:post_type><![CDATA[post]]></wp:post_type>
		<wp:post_password><![CDATA[]]></wp:post_password>
		<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
										<category domain="category" nicename="design"><![CDATA[Design]]></category>
						<wp:postmeta>
		<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[_pingme]]></wp:meta_key>
		<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[1]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
							</item>
					<item>
		<title><![CDATA[Typography for interfaces, not posters]]></title>
		<link>https://wpaxiom.com/typography-for-interfaces/</link>
		<pubDate>Thu, 03 Sep 2026 19:02:12 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://wpaxiom.com/typography-for-interfaces/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Interface type is read in glances. That single fact changes most of the decisions people copy from print, and it is why a specimen that looks beautiful at poster size can feel unusable in a sidebar.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Line height does more work than size</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Most "this text is too small" complaints turn out to be line height problems. At 15px with a 1.2 line height, a two line label reads as a solid block. The same text at 1.6 reads instantly, and nobody asks for a bigger font.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Headings run the other way. A 48px heading at 1.6 falls apart into separate lines that no longer read as one thought. Tighten as the size grows.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Numbers need their own rule</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Anything that changes while the reader is looking at it needs <code>font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums</code>. Counters, timers, live metrics, table columns. With proportional figures a 4 is narrower than a 0, so a number that updates every frame shifts sideways the whole time it animates. It reads as a rendering bug, and people report it as one.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Two families is plenty</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">One family for headings, one for everything else. A third arrives with a specific job, usually code, and then quietly starts appearing in captions. Each family is a font file the reader waits for, so the cost is not only visual.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[Interface type is read in glances. That single fact changes most of the decisions people copy from print.]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>270</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-09-03 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-03 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:post_modified><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified>
		<wp:post_modified_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[typography-for-interfaces]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
		<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
		<wp:post_type><![CDATA[post]]></wp:post_type>
		<wp:post_password><![CDATA[]]></wp:post_password>
		<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
										<category domain="category" nicename="design"><![CDATA[Design]]></category>
						<wp:postmeta>
		<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[_pingme]]></wp:meta_key>
		<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[1]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
							</item>
					<item>
		<title><![CDATA[Why your grid collapses on tablet]]></title>
		<link>https://wpaxiom.com/grid-collapses-tablet/</link>
		<pubDate>Wed, 02 Sep 2026 19:02:12 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://wpaxiom.com/grid-collapses-tablet/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">The intermediate breakpoint is where most grids quietly fall apart, and the fix is usually to remove a step rather than add one. A three column layout that becomes two at 900px and one at 600px has three states to design, three to test and three to keep in sync.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Three ways to write the same collapse</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/tabs -->
<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/tab-panel {"tabId":"tab-1","label":"Media queries"} -->
<div class="wp-block-axiom-blocks-tab-panel axiom-blocks-tab-panel" data-tab-id="tab-1"><span class="axiom-blocks-tab-panel__label">Media queries</span><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.65"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:16px;line-height:1.65">Explicit and predictable. You decide every state, which is either the point or the problem depending on how many components you own. Two breakpoints per grid multiplied across forty components is a lot of arithmetic nobody wants to audit.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:axiom-blocks/tab-panel -->

<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/tab-panel {"tabId":"tab-2","label":"Auto-fit"} -->
<div class="wp-block-axiom-blocks-tab-panel axiom-blocks-tab-panel" data-tab-id="tab-2"><span class="axiom-blocks-tab-panel__label">Auto-fit</span><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.65"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:16px;line-height:1.65">One line of CSS handles every width, and the column count follows from a minimum card size rather than a device. The catch is that you give up control of the intermediate state, so a card that needs to be either full width or one third never gets to say so.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:axiom-blocks/tab-panel -->

<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/tab-panel {"tabId":"tab-3","label":"Container"} -->
<div class="wp-block-axiom-blocks-tab-panel axiom-blocks-tab-panel" data-tab-id="tab-3"><span class="axiom-blocks-tab-panel__label">Container</span><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.65"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:16px;line-height:1.65">The card responds to the space it was given rather than the viewport, which is what you actually meant every time you wrote a media query. Worth the migration for anything that appears in more than one column width.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div>
<!-- /wp:axiom-blocks/tab-panel -->
<!-- /wp:axiom-blocks/tabs -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Whichever you pick, preserve source order. A grid that visually reorders at one breakpoint and not another produces a tab sequence that makes no sense to anyone navigating by keyboard.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[The intermediate breakpoint is where most grids quietly fall apart, and the fix is usually to remove a step rather than add one.]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>271</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-09-02 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-02 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:post_modified><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified>
		<wp:post_modified_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[grid-collapses-tablet]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
		<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
