										<category domain="category" nicename="layout"><![CDATA[Layout]]></category>
						<wp:postmeta>
		<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[_pingme]]></wp:meta_key>
		<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[1]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
							</item>
					<item>
		<title><![CDATA[Container queries in practice]]></title>
		<link>https://wpaxiom.com/container-queries-practice/</link>
		<pubDate>Tue, 01 Sep 2026 19:02:12 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://wpaxiom.com/container-queries-practice/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">After a year of shipping them, the useful pattern is narrower than the demos suggest, and more reliable. Container queries are not a replacement for media queries. They answer a different question.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Where they earn their keep</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">One component, several widths. A card that appears in a three column grid, a sidebar and a full width hero used to need three modifier classes and a rule about which one wins. With a container query it needs none, because the card measures its own box.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Page level decisions stay with media queries. Whether the sidebar exists at all is a viewport question, not a container one.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">The rule that catches everyone</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">An element cannot query itself. Declaring <code>container-type</code> on the card and then querying it from inside the card does nothing, silently. You need a wrapper. Every team hits this once, spends twenty minutes on it, and never hits it again.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Also worth knowing: <code>container-type: inline-size</code> creates a containment context, which means the element can no longer be sized by its own children in that axis. Setting it on something that relied on intrinsic width will collapse it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Try it locally</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">The sample project has the wrapper pattern already set up, with one card rendered at four widths on the same page.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/copy-to-clipboard {"textToCopy":"npx create-sable-demo container-queries","buttonText":"Copy command","successText":"Copied","buttonStyle":"filled","buttonColor":"#7C3AED","buttonTextColor":"#FFFFFF","borderRadius":"10px","fontFamily":"'DM Sans', system-ui, sans-serif","fontSize":"15px"} -->
<div class="wp-block-axiom-blocks-copy-to-clipboard axiom-blocks-copy-to-clipboard"><div class="axiom-blocks-copy-to-clipboard__input-row"><span class="axiom-blocks-copy-to-clipboard__input">npx create-sable-demo container-queries</span><span class="axiom-blocks-copy-to-clipboard__button">Copy command</span></div></div>
<!-- /wp:axiom-blocks/copy-to-clipboard -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[After a year of shipping them, the useful pattern is narrower than the demos suggest, and more reliable.]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>272</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-09-01 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-01 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:post_modified><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified>
		<wp:post_modified_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[container-queries-practice]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
		<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
		<wp:post_type><![CDATA[post]]></wp:post_type>
		<wp:post_password><![CDATA[]]></wp:post_password>
		<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
										<category domain="category" nicename="layout"><![CDATA[Layout]]></category>
						<wp:postmeta>
		<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[_pingme]]></wp:meta_key>
		<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[1]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
							</item>
					<item>
		<title><![CDATA[A layout is a set of constraints]]></title>
		<link>https://wpaxiom.com/layout-is-constraints/</link>
		<pubDate>Mon, 31 Aug 2026 19:02:12 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://wpaxiom.com/layout-is-constraints/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Describing a layout by what it refuses to do turns out to be more portable than describing what it looks like. "Never more than 1160px, never less than 20px of breathing room, never an orphaned last column" survives a redesign. A screenshot does not.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Write the constraints down</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Ours live in one file next to the theme config, in plain sentences. It is read more often than the design file, because a developer resolving an edge case at 4pm wants a rule, not a canvas they have to interpret.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">The rule that has saved the most time is the dullest one: the page has exactly one left edge and one right edge, and every section shares them. Headers, footers and full bleed bands included. Once that is stated, a whole category of "this looks slightly off" bug reports stops arriving.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Constraints you cannot enforce are wishes</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">We had a rule about maximum line length that nothing checked. It was broken in nine places within a quarter. The rules that held were the ones expressed as a token, a lint rule or a component that simply would not render the wrong thing.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[Describing a layout by what it refuses to do turns out to be more portable than describing what it looks like.]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>273</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-08-31 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-08-31 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:post_modified><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified>
		<wp:post_modified_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[layout-is-constraints]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
		<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
		<wp:post_type><![CDATA[post]]></wp:post_type>
		<wp:post_password><![CDATA[]]></wp:post_password>
		<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
