		<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[1]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
							</item>
					<item>
		<title><![CDATA[Server rendering is back, quietly]]></title>
		<link>https://wpaxiom.com/server-rendering-back/</link>
		<pubDate>Sun, 30 Aug 2026 19:02:12 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://wpaxiom.com/server-rendering-back/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Nobody announced it and there was no migration guide, but a lot of teams have moved rendering back to the server over the last two years. The reasons are less ideological than the arguments around them suggest.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">The bundle was never the whole cost</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Shipping less JavaScript helps, but the bigger win was removing a whole class of state. A page rendered on the server has one source of truth. A page hydrated on the client has two, and the interesting bugs all live in the gap between them.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Our filter UI is the clearest example. It submits a form. The server validates the input against a fixed set of allowed values and returns a rendered result. The client script upgrades that to an in place swap when JavaScript is available, and when it is not, the form still works. There is no second implementation of the query.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">What got worse</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Local development got slower, and the feedback loop for a purely visual change now runs through a server round trip that used to be instant. Caching also became a design problem rather than a deployment detail, which is a real cost and not one to wave away.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[The pendulum swung, but what returned is not what left. The differences are the interesting part.]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>274</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-08-30 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-08-30 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:post_modified><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified>
		<wp:post_modified_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[server-rendering-back]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
		<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
		<wp:post_type><![CDATA[post]]></wp:post_type>
		<wp:post_password><![CDATA[]]></wp:post_password>
		<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
										<category domain="category" nicename="engineering"><![CDATA[Engineering]]></category>
						<wp:postmeta>
		<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[_pingme]]></wp:meta_key>
		<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[1]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
							</item>
					<item>
		<title><![CDATA[Progressive enhancement without the sermon]]></title>
		<link>https://wpaxiom.com/progressive-enhancement/</link>
		<pubDate>Sat, 29 Aug 2026 19:02:12 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://wpaxiom.com/progressive-enhancement/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">The argument usually arrives wrapped in a moral lecture about users without JavaScript, which is a shame, because the practical case is stronger and much shorter.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">It is about failure, not preference</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Almost nobody turns JavaScript off on purpose. Scripts fail anyway: a timeout on hotel wifi, a blocked CDN, an extension that breaks a global, a parse error from a browser two versions behind. Each is rare. Together they are not, and every one of them lands on a user who has no idea what happened.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/notice {"noticeType":"warning","title":"A cheap test","message":"Disable JavaScript and load your three most important pages. Not to support that as a mode, but to see what your users get on the day your script does not arrive.","accentColor":"#B45309","bgColor":"#FEF6E7","titleColor":"#17131F","textColor":"#4C4658","borderRadius":"12px","showIcon":true,"iconSlug":"alert-triangle"} -->
<div class="wp-block-axiom-blocks-notice"><div class="ab-notice__content"><strong class="ab-notice__title">A cheap test</strong><div class="ab-notice__message">Disable JavaScript and load your three most important pages. Not to support that as a mode, but to see what your users get on the day your script does not arrive.</div></div></div>
<!-- /wp:axiom-blocks/notice -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">The shape that works</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Build the thing that works with a form and a page load. Then intercept it. The upgraded path reuses the same endpoint and the same validation, so there is one implementation and the fallback cannot rot, because it is the thing you built first.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">This falls apart if you build the rich version first and retrofit a fallback. The retrofit gets tested once, then drifts, and two releases later it is broken and nobody knows.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[A form that works before the script loads is not nostalgia. It is the cheapest reliability you will ever buy.]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>275</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-08-29 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-08-29 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:post_modified><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified>
		<wp:post_modified_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[progressive-enhancement]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
		<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
		<wp:post_type><![CDATA[post]]></wp:post_type>
		<wp:post_password><![CDATA[]]></wp:post_password>
		<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
										<category domain="category" nicename="engineering"><![CDATA[Engineering]]></category>
						<wp:postmeta>
		<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[_pingme]]></wp:meta_key>
		<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[1]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
							</item>
					<item>
		<title><![CDATA[Caching is a naming problem]]></title>
		<link>https://wpaxiom.com/caching-naming-problem/</link>
		<pubDate>Fri, 28 Aug 2026 19:02:12 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[admin]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://wpaxiom.com/caching-naming-problem/</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/table-of-contents {"eyebrow":"On this page","markerType":"numbered","bgColor":"#FFFFFF","borderColor":"#EBE9F2","borderRadius":"16px","activeColor":"#7C3AED","markerColor":"#7C3AED","maxWidth":"100%","levelH2":true,"levelH3":false} /-->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Phil Karlton's line about the two hard things gets quoted as a joke. It is closer to a specification. Almost every caching incident we have had was a naming failure wearing a different hat.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">A key is a promise</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">The key says: everything that affects this result is captured here. Get it right and invalidation is arithmetic. Miss one input and you have a bug that only appears for the second user, in a language you do not read, on a page nobody on the team visits.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Our worst one omitted the user's timezone from a key for a rendered date. It survived three months because everybody who tested it sat in the same office.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Derive keys, do not write them</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"17px","lineHeight":"1.7"},"color":{"text":"#3A3546"}}} -->
<p class="has-text-color" style="color:#3A3546;font-size:17px;line-height:1.7">Hand written keys go stale the moment somebody adds a parameter and forgets the key. Hashing the actual input structure means the key changes when the inputs change, without anyone remembering to update it. It costs a little clarity in logs and buys back a category of incident.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading {"level":2,"style":{"typography":{"fontFamily":"'Space Grotesk', sans-serif","fontSize":"24px","fontWeight":"600","lineHeight":"1.25","letterSpacing":"-0.018em"},"color":{"text":"#17131F"},"spacing":{"margin":{"top":"38px","bottom":"12px"}}}} -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading has-text-color" style="color:#17131F;font-family:'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;font-size:24px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-0.018em;line-height:1.25;margin-top:38px;margin-bottom:12px">Questions we get asked</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/accordion {"firstItemOpen":true} -->
<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/accordion-item {"title":"How long should a cache live?"} -->
<details class="wp-block-axiom-blocks-accordion-item ab-accordion__item"><summary class="ab-accordion__header"><span class="ab-accordion__title">How long should a cache live?</span></summary><div class="ab-accordion__body"><div class="ab-accordion__body-inner"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.62"}}} -->
<p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.62">Long enough that it helps, short enough that a mistake expires on its own. We default to five minutes for anything derived from user input and an hour for anything derived from published content, then adjust when the numbers say otherwise.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div></details>
<!-- /wp:axiom-blocks/accordion-item -->

<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/accordion-item {"title":"Should I cache per user?"} -->
<details class="wp-block-axiom-blocks-accordion-item ab-accordion__item"><summary class="ab-accordion__header"><span class="ab-accordion__title">Should I cache per user?</span></summary><div class="ab-accordion__body"><div class="ab-accordion__body-inner"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.62"}}} -->
<p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.62">Usually not. A per user cache has a hit rate close to zero and all of the invalidation cost. Cache the shared part, then compose the personal part on top of it.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div></details>
<!-- /wp:axiom-blocks/accordion-item -->

<!-- wp:axiom-blocks/accordion-item {"title":"What about cache stampedes?"} -->
<details class="wp-block-axiom-blocks-accordion-item ab-accordion__item"><summary class="ab-accordion__header"><span class="ab-accordion__title">What about cache stampedes?</span></summary><div class="ab-accordion__body"><div class="ab-accordion__body-inner"><!-- wp:paragraph {"style":{"typography":{"fontSize":"16px","lineHeight":"1.62"}}} -->
<p style="font-size:16px;line-height:1.62">Serve the stale value while one request recomputes in the background. Readers get an answer that is a few seconds old instead of a queue, and the database sees one query instead of two thousand.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph --></div></div></details>
<!-- /wp:axiom-blocks/accordion-item -->
<!-- /wp:axiom-blocks/accordion -->]]></content:encoded>
		<excerpt:encoded><![CDATA[Almost every cache bug reduces to two things sharing a key that should not, or splitting one that should not.]]></excerpt:encoded>
		<wp:post_id>276</wp:post_id>
		<wp:post_date><![CDATA[2026-08-28 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date>
		<wp:post_date_gmt><![CDATA[2026-08-28 19:02:12]]></wp:post_date_gmt>
		<wp:post_modified><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified>
		<wp:post_modified_gmt><![CDATA[2026-09-06 06:36:40]]></wp:post_modified_gmt>
		<wp:comment_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:comment_status>
		<wp:ping_status><![CDATA[open]]></wp:ping_status>
		<wp:post_name><![CDATA[caching-naming-problem]]></wp:post_name>
		<wp:status><![CDATA[publish]]></wp:status>
		<wp:post_parent>0</wp:post_parent>
		<wp:menu_order>0</wp:menu_order>
		<wp:post_type><![CDATA[post]]></wp:post_type>
		<wp:post_password><![CDATA[]]></wp:post_password>
		<wp:is_sticky>0</wp:is_sticky>
										<category domain="category" nicename="engineering"><![CDATA[Engineering]]></category>
